WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.197 --> 00:00:02.780
(gentle music)

00:00:09.340 --> 00:00:10.960
- I remember my first experience

00:00:10.960 --> 00:00:14.280
of seeing this amazing production of art

00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:16.030
that was different than what I had seen

00:00:16.030 --> 00:00:17.770
and it was such a personal expression

00:00:17.770 --> 00:00:19.200
by the person making it.

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:20.950
They may or may not have had language,

00:00:20.950 --> 00:00:23.530
they may or may not been
able to communicate with me,

00:00:23.530 --> 00:00:24.530
but they're making this work.

00:00:24.530 --> 00:00:27.603
That's really a window into their soul.

00:00:29.900 --> 00:00:32.160
When you ask someone
who's been disenfranchised

00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:35.360
their entire life to tell me your story

00:00:35.360 --> 00:00:37.033
it's amazing how the door opens.

00:00:43.133 --> 00:00:46.300
(upbeat violin music)

00:00:53.550 --> 00:00:55.630
Creative Growth Art Center
is the oldest and largest

00:00:55.630 --> 00:00:58.610
center for artists with
disabilities in the world.

00:00:58.610 --> 00:01:02.240
When we were founded, the
idea of disability, then,

00:01:02.240 --> 00:01:05.530
was so radically different
than what it is now.

00:01:05.530 --> 00:01:07.490
We didn't come out of a hospital setting

00:01:07.490 --> 00:01:10.370
or we didn't come out of a
vocational training program,

00:01:10.370 --> 00:01:12.700
we came out of some
artists getting together,

00:01:12.700 --> 00:01:16.310
in a home in Oakland, putting
paint on a table in the garage

00:01:16.310 --> 00:01:18.170
and just saying, this is what artists do,

00:01:18.170 --> 00:01:20.983
and this is how we can
change the social fabric.

00:01:25.270 --> 00:01:27.260
From those, kind of, humble beginnings,

00:01:27.260 --> 00:01:30.630
we now serve 162 artists with disabilities

00:01:30.630 --> 00:01:32.720
in our studio, every week.

00:01:32.720 --> 00:01:34.140
It's a very big space.

00:01:34.140 --> 00:01:37.710
Within that, you'll find almost
every possible expression

00:01:37.710 --> 00:01:38.980
of visual art.

00:01:38.980 --> 00:01:41.220
You'll see the rug making
area, the wood shop,

00:01:41.220 --> 00:01:43.480
the ceramic studio, the fashion area,

00:01:43.480 --> 00:01:44.890
the painting and drawing area

00:01:44.890 --> 00:01:46.240
and everyone's working together

00:01:46.240 --> 00:01:48.610
and everyone can see each other.

00:01:48.610 --> 00:01:50.320
It's different than most places

00:01:50.320 --> 00:01:53.740
and some people don't believe it.

00:01:53.740 --> 00:01:56.470
Some people think, like,
you must have doctors

00:01:56.470 --> 00:01:58.070
and you must have all these other things

00:01:58.070 --> 00:02:00.300
and how do you deal with
the studio everyday.

00:02:00.300 --> 00:02:03.263
It's like, no, you know, art is the common language.

00:02:04.130 --> 00:02:06.390
And a lot of our folks
don't verbalize or speak

00:02:06.390 --> 00:02:07.600
or they speak sign language,

00:02:07.600 --> 00:02:10.003
but art is the language
that moves us forward.

00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:16.670
- This is called the
Oil Red Sea Number One.

00:02:16.670 --> 00:02:18.773
Red is the only color I can see.

00:02:20.520 --> 00:02:23.140
- [Tom] You look at work of
an artist like Dan Miller,

00:02:23.140 --> 00:02:24.630
ya know, he's pretty much non-verbal.

00:02:24.630 --> 00:02:27.010
His mother tried to encourage him to speak

00:02:27.010 --> 00:02:29.860
by telling him how to spell
words when he was a boy,

00:02:29.860 --> 00:02:31.140
every night, and never spoke them

00:02:31.140 --> 00:02:33.214
until he started to draw them.

00:02:33.214 --> 00:02:36.400
- A light goes click, click, click.

00:02:38.653 --> 00:02:41.170
- [Tom] Look at an artist
like Monica Valentine,

00:02:41.170 --> 00:02:45.580
she takes pins and she takes
sequins and colored beads,

00:02:45.580 --> 00:02:47.400
and she strings those together on the pin

00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:49.830
and she puts them into styrofoam forms

00:02:49.830 --> 00:02:52.723
to form these optically
charged sculptures.

00:02:53.600 --> 00:02:57.440
When you realize that
Monica has prosthetic eyes

00:02:57.440 --> 00:03:00.410
and can't see color or see anything,

00:03:00.410 --> 00:03:02.793
it moves into this whole other range.

00:03:04.030 --> 00:03:06.980
We want their own voice,
we want it as pure

00:03:06.980 --> 00:03:09.350
and personalized as it can be.

00:03:09.350 --> 00:03:12.950
And 45 years later, it continues to work.

00:03:12.950 --> 00:03:15.479
How's that building coming along, Pete?

00:03:15.479 --> 00:03:17.029
- Slowly.
- It's looking good.

00:03:22.420 --> 00:03:24.850
The record of success
that Creative Growth has

00:03:24.850 --> 00:03:26.810
is phenomenal, it's off the charts

00:03:26.810 --> 00:03:28.790
in terms of what an art school

00:03:28.790 --> 00:03:30.440
or any other, sort of, enterprise

00:03:30.440 --> 00:03:32.363
would consider to be acceptable.

00:03:33.280 --> 00:03:36.340
We have three artists with work
in the Museum of Modern Art

00:03:36.340 --> 00:03:37.460
permanent collection in New York,

00:03:37.460 --> 00:03:39.920
in the San Francisco Museum
of Modern Art collection.

00:03:39.920 --> 00:03:42.240
And, this year, we have
the first two people

00:03:42.240 --> 00:03:43.570
with developmental disabilities,

00:03:43.570 --> 00:03:45.700
they have work in the Venice Bienalle.

00:03:45.700 --> 00:03:48.880
The Venice Bienalle is the
most important, prestigious,

00:03:48.880 --> 00:03:51.493
by invitation-only
exhibition in the world.

00:03:52.650 --> 00:03:54.190
Their work is sought after.

00:03:54.190 --> 00:03:56.770
Ya know, a piece is done,
and there's a waiting list.

00:03:56.770 --> 00:03:58.940
Rosina, I haven't even said
hello to you, yet, today.

00:03:58.940 --> 00:03:59.940
How are you?
- Good.

00:03:59.940 --> 00:04:01.830
- To be able to work at a place like this

00:04:01.830 --> 00:04:04.053
is beyond anything I had hoped for.

00:04:04.900 --> 00:04:07.627
I get to work with
phenomenally talented people,

00:04:07.627 --> 00:04:11.160
and to see how they
negotiate the day everyday,

00:04:11.160 --> 00:04:15.130
how they get around obstacles,
how they continue to say yes,

00:04:15.130 --> 00:04:16.600
when everyone is telling them no,

00:04:16.600 --> 00:04:17.570
that's an amazing thing.

00:04:17.570 --> 00:04:20.623
That's a life lesson I get
reminded of every single day.

00:04:21.526 --> 00:04:24.193
(gentle music)

